      <workList xml:id="workDesc_b409f746">
         <work xml:id="work_ida8fbcec2">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_9aedff6b">B2:307</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_id3d4a6a3c" xml:lang="de">Passions-Cantate</title>
            <title type="alternative" xml:id="title_1ba50f6e" xml:lang="de">Der aus Liebe sterbende Jesus: Das Haupt erblaßt</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_25264b74">
               <persName role="composer" xml:id="persName_id8021618d">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
            </contributor>
            <history xml:id="history_id5b0ce2ff">
               <p xml:id="idm61">The Passion cantata has presumably been lost. It appears only in the Breitkopf catalogue of 1761, thus suggesting that it may have been acquired by the publishers from the estate of <persName xml:id="idm62" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Carl Gotthelf Gerlach</persName>, the cantor of Neukirche in Leipzig who had died earlier that year. In that case it was probably performed there and might have been commissioned by Gerlach. It is possible that the cantata was one of his early works mentioned in Scheibe’s autobiography of 1740 (Mattheson's <rend xml:id="idm63" fontstyle="italic">Grundlage</rend>,1740; SchW C5:001). It is not listed in the Breitkopf auction catalogue of 1836. The instrumentation of the cantata is somewhat unusual when compared to the majority of Scheibe's works: it includes both flûtes d'amour (occurs also in SchW B2:017) as well as the five-stringed violoncello piccolo, an instrument which <persName xml:id="idm64" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann Sebastian Bach</persName> also employed in some of his cantatas of the 1720s.</p>
            </history>
            <langUsage xml:id="langUsage_312ad5f9">
               <language xml:id="en">English</language>
               <language xml:id="de">German</language>
            </langUsage>
